{
  "title": "Explore: SQLite-based local-first storage for client-side search",
  "description": "Idea from Gregor (2026-06-11): consider replacing the DB \
setup with SQLite so iOS can do local searches, acknowledging this probably \
requires more complicated sync logic that may not be worth it.\n\
\n\
This is an exploration issue — produce an analysis doc, not an implementation. \
Questions to answer:\n\
\n\
1. What's the actual goal — full local-first (offline writes + sync) vs just \
fast local search? iOS already caches recipe summaries (RecipeCacheStore.swift \
via CoreData) and could plausibly get local search over that cache WITHOUT \
touching the server's Postgres/Diesel setup. Quantify how far the cheap \
version gets.\n\
2. If full local-first: survey the sync approaches (e.g. the existing \
syncRecipes/syncItems delta protocol extended to full fidelity, vs \
CRDT/replication frameworks, vs SQLite-replication tools in the current \
ecosystem — check what's state of the art at implementation time). Be honest \
about conflict handling complexity for a single-user multi-device setup, which \
is much easier than general multi-user.\n\
3. Server impact: Diesel works with SQLite, but migrations, JSONB usage, and \
full-text search would all need auditing. Is server-side Postgres->SQLite even \
necessary for any of the client goals, or is this two unrelated decisions?\n\
\n\
Recommendation should weigh against the standing constraint that the owner \
doesn't want heavy sync-logic maintenance. Plausible outcome: keep Postgres \
server-side, extend the existing sync endpoints + on-device cache for local \
search, skip generic replication.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 4,
